[CourseId "scandinavian-black"]
[CourseTitle "The Scandinavian Defence"]
[StudentColour "black"]
[Tagline "The simple answer to 1.e4, as Black."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "vs-nc3 vs-nf3 vs-blackmar vs-no-capture vs-anti-scandinavian queen-safety light-squares c5-break e5-break"]
[ChapterId "ch3-other-third-moves"]
[ChapterTitle "Third moves other than 3.Nc3"]
[DefaultTags "vs-nf3"]

{After 2...Qxd5 almost everyone plays 3.Nc3, because it develops with a hit on the queen and there is no reason not to. This chapter is the rest: White develops something else first, or takes the centre first, or offers a queen trade. In every case Black plays the same setup, and in most cases the game becomes chapter 1 by move six.

## The one choice

Against 3.Nf3 the knight comes to f6, and the pin on g4 is refused in a sentence: it is a good move and it commits the bishop before White has shown where the knight on b1 is going, and if that knight goes to c3 with a hit on the queen the bishop on g4 is a move Black may wish it had back. The knight to f6 keeps every option, and the pin comes a move later.

## What the others give up

3.d4 takes the centre first and usually transposes. 3.Be2 is quiet and blocks nothing, and it leaves the pawn on g2 to the queen: this is the one line in the course where the queen goes and takes something, because it can come back to g6 a pawn up with nothing to answer for. 3.Qf3 offers a queen trade, and Black takes it: with the queens off there is no tempo to lose and the structure with the pawn on c6 plays itself. 3.d3 is the small centre, and the pawn to c6 and the bishop to f5 are the answer, as against every quiet move in this course.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Declining the queen trade after 3.Qf3, which keeps the queen on d5 as a target for a knight that has not yet moved. Pinning on g4 before the knight on g8 has moved. Playing ...e6 before the bishop is out. Declining a free pawn on g2 after 3.Be2 in order to develop, when the queen can take it and come home. And chasing the main line when a quiet move has been played: against 3.d3 there is nothing to punish, and the right answer is to develop and be comfortable.}
1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nf3
{White develops the other knight first, keeping the option of Nc3 with a hit on the queen for a move later. [%weight 7]}
( 3. d4
{White takes the centre first and decides about the knights afterwards. [%weight 6]}
3... Nf6 {Develop. [%tags development]} ( 3... e5?!
{4.Nf3 hits the pawn and 4.dxe5 simply takes it, with the queen loose in the middle either way.}
) ( 3... Nc6?!
{It blocks the pawn that gives the queen its square, and 4.Nf3 followed by Nc3 hits the queen with no c7 to go to.}
) 4. Nc3 {The hit on the queen. [%weight 7]} ( 4. Nf3
{Develop the other knight first. [%weight 6]} 4... c6
{The structure, and this is the 3.Nf3 line of this chapter by another order. [%tags queen-safety] [%plan The 3.Nf3 line above takes it from here: 5.Nc3 Qd6 is chapter 1, and 5.Be2 Bf5 is the line with the c-pawn.] [%line 3.d4 with 4.Nf3, the same position]}
) 4... Qd6 {The modern square, exactly as in chapter 1. [%tags queen-safety]} ( 4... Qa5!?
{The classical square, with its two tricks to know. The course keeps to d6.} ) (
4... Qd8?! {The whole tempo given back for nothing.} ) 5. Nf3
{Develop, and this is chapter 1's position. [%weight 8]} 5... c6
{The structure, and the game is chapter 1 from here. [%tags queen-safety] [%plan Chapter 1 takes it from here.] [%line 3.d4 with 4.Nc3, into the main line]}
) ( 3. Be2
{A quiet developing move that blocks nothing, threatens nothing, and leaves the pawn on g2 to the queen. [%weight 4]}
3... Qxg2
{Take it. The queen is chased once and lands on g6, and Black is a pawn up with nothing to answer for. This is the one line in the course where the queen goes hunting, because the pawn is free. [%tags tactics]}
( 3... Nf6?!
{Sound development, and it declines a pawn that can be taken safely. Against a move that hangs something, take it.}
) ( 3... Nc6?! {Also sound, and the same pawn is still on g2 waiting to be taken.} )
4. Bf3
{The bishop hits the queen, which is the only thing White gets for the pawn. [%weight 9]}
4... Qg6
{To g6, where the bishop cannot reach it, it watches c2, and it is one move from home. A pawn up. [%tags queen-safety] [%plan Meet Nc3 with the pawn to c6 so the knight has no b5, then the bishop to f5, the knight to d7 and the pawn to e6, and the king castles when the queenside is quiet. The pawn is kept and the position is the usual one with an extra pawn.] [%line 3.Be2 and the pawn on g2]}
( 4... Qg5!?
{Also keeps the pawn, and 5.d4 hits the queen again. On g6 it is not asked to move a third time.}
) ( 4... Qxf3? {5.Qxf3 and the queen has been given for a bishop.} ) ( 4... Bg4?
{The queen is still on g2: 5.Bxg2 takes it, and 5...Bxd1 6.Kxd1 leaves White a piece up.}
) ) ( 3. Qf3
{White offers a queen trade, hoping Black declines and keeps the queen as a target. [%weight 4]}
3... Qxf3
{Take. With the queens off there is no tempo to lose on the queen, and Black's structure plays itself. [%tags queen-safety]}
( 3... Qe6+?! {4.Be2 blocks with a developing move and the queen has to move again.} ) (
3... Qd8?!
{It declines a trade that costs Black nothing and keeps the queen at home for a whole move.}
) 4. Nxf3 {The knight takes back and is developed. [%weight 9]} 4... Nf6
{Develop. [%tags development]} ( 4... Bg4!?
{Fine, and the knight first keeps the bishop's choice open.} ) 5. d4
{White takes the centre. [%weight 8]} 5... Bf5
{The bishop comes out. With no queens the trade on d3 that follows is nothing to fear. [%tags light-squares]}
( 5... e6?! {Shut in. The bishop comes out first.} ) 6. Bd3
{White offers to trade the bishops. [%weight 7]} 6... Bxd3
{Take. The trade leaves White's pawns doubled on the d-file, which is a target for the rest of the game. [%tags light-squares]}
( 6... Bg6?!
{It keeps the bishop and lets White keep the structure. Taking first is what doubles White's pawns.}
) 7. cxd3
{White takes back with the c-pawn, towards the centre, and the d-pawns are doubled. [%weight 8]}
7... e6
{The pawn goes. Black has a healthy structure against doubled pawns and no queens to worry about. [%tags development] [%plan Develop the bishop to e7 or d6, castle, and put the knights on d5 and c6 in front of the doubled pawns. The ending is already better for Black.] [%line 3.Qf3 and the queens off]}
( 7... Nc6!? {Fine. The pawn first frees the bishop on f8.} ) ) ( 3. d3
{The small centre. White keeps the pawn back and develops behind it. [%weight 3]} 3... Nf6
{Develop. [%tags development]} ( 3... e5!?
{Sound with White's pawn on d3. The course keeps to the structure with c6.} ) 4. Nf3
{Develop. [%weight 8]} 4... c6 {The structure. [%tags queen-safety]} ( 4... Bg4!?
{Fine. The pawn first gives the queen its squares.} ) 5. Be2 {Develop. [%weight 7]}
5... Bf5
{The bishop comes out, and against the small centre it has the whole diagonal. [%tags light-squares]}
( 5... e6?! {Shut in. The bishop comes out first.} ) 6. O-O {White castles. [%weight 8]}
6... e6
{The pawn goes, and Black has the full structure against a centre that never came forward. [%tags development] [%plan Bishop to e7 or d6, castle, knight to d7, and then the pawn to e5 to take the centre White declined.] [%line 3.d3 and the small centre]}
( 6... e5!?
{Also good with the pawn on d3. The modest move keeps to the structure the course teaches.}
) ) 3... Nf6
{Develop. The knight takes its square and keeps the bishop's choice open until White's other knight has shown where it is going. [%tags development]}
( 3... Bg4!?
{The pin is a good move and it is refused under the fork rule: it commits the bishop before White's knight on b1 has moved, and 4.Be2 followed by Nc3 asks the queen and the bishop questions at once.}
) ( 3... c6!? {Fine and it usually transposes. The knight first keeps every option.} ) (
3... e5?!
{4.Nc3 hits the queen and the pawn on e5 is loose behind it, so Black is answering threats from move four.}
) 4. d4 {White takes the centre. [%weight 8]} 4... c6
{The structure, before the knight on c3 arrives to hit the queen. The queen already has c7 and d6 to choose from. [%tags queen-safety]}
( 4... Bg4!?
{Fine, and 5.Nc3 hits the queen while the bishop is committed. The pawn first gives the queen its squares.}
) ( 4... Nc6?!
{5.Nc3 hits the queen and the knight on c6 blocks the pawn that would have given it a square.}
) 5. Nc3
{The hit on the queen, and this is chapter 1's position with the moves in a different order. [%weight 8]}
( 5. Be2 {White develops without hitting the queen, and castles next. [%weight 5]}
5... Bf5
{The bishop comes out before the pawn goes to e6. With no knight on c3 the queen on d5 is comfortable and the bishop has the whole diagonal. [%tags light-squares]}
( 5... Bg4!?
{Also good. With the queen still on d5 the bishop on f5 covers the b1 knight's route and cannot be asked a question by h3.}
) ( 5... e6?! {Shut in. The bishop comes out first.} ) 6. O-O {White castles. [%weight 8]}
6... e6 {The pawn goes with the bishop out. [%tags development]} ( 6... Nbd7!?
{Fine. The pawn first frees the bishop on f8.} ) 7. c4
{White hits the queen with the c-pawn and takes space. [%weight 6]} 7... Qd8
{Home to d8. The queen has done its work and the pawn on c4 has left a hole on d4 for Black's pieces to use later. [%tags queen-safety]}
( 7... Qd6?! {8.c5 hits it again and the queen has spent three moves to reach d7 or c7.} )
( 7... Qa5?!
{It looks active and it is loose: 8.Nc3 and then Bd2 hit it again, and the queen has spent three moves going nowhere.}
) 8. Nc3 {Develop. [%weight 8]} 8... Be7
{Develop and prepare to castle. [%tags development]} ( 8... Nbd7!?
{Also fine. The bishop first keeps castling one move away.} ) 9. Be3
{Develop. [%weight 7]} 9... O-O {Castle. [%tags king-safety]} 10. Qb3
{The queen comes out to hit the pawn on b7 and support the c-pawn. [%weight 6]} 10... Qb6
{Offer the trade. The queen on b6 defends b7, hits d4, and if White trades the position is level with the better pawns for Black. [%tags queen-safety] [%plan If the queens come off, put a rook on d8 and the knight on d7, and play against the pawn on d4. If they stay on, the knight goes to d7 and then b6 or f6, and the pawn to c5 hits the centre.] [%line 3.Nf3 with 5.Be2 and the c-pawn]}
( 10... Qc7!? {Sound, and the queen on b6 does more: it defends the pawn and hits one.} )
( 10... b6?!
{It weakens the queenside pawns for a threat that was easy to meet with a piece.} ) )
5... Qd6
{The modern square, and the game is chapter 1 from here. [%tags queen-safety] [%plan Chapter 1 takes it from here: the bishop to g4 or f5 before the pawn goes to e6, then Be7 and castling, with the queen going to c7 or d8 when it is asked.] [%line 3.Nf3, back into the main line]}
