[ChapterId "ch6-tactics-and-plans"]
[ChapterTitle "Typical tactics and plans"]
[DefaultTags "tactics"]

{Six positions, each running through a line from an earlier chapter and stopping on the move that matters. Play the line and find the last move yourself. Three of them are about the queen, because the queen out early is the one thing every opponent knows about this opening, and knowing where it goes when it is hit is most of what there is to know.

## What each one is about

The first is the knight to b5. It hits the queen and eyes c7, and the answer is the retreat that covers c7 rather than the active square that leaves it.

The second is the check on b5. The pawn that was going to c6 anyway blocks it with tempo, and the bishop has to move again.

The third is the knight jump. With the queens off and White's king stuck on the open file, a knight comes to b4 and the fork on c2 is in the air.

The fourth is the pin. The whole structure is built and every piece is placed, and the bishop on g5 is still pinning the knight; the knight to d5 steps out of it, offers a trade that suits Black, and takes the queen out of the bishop's sights.

The fifth is the break with the c-pawn. The bishop on e4 has been hit and has gone home, the d-pawn has one pawn behind it and nothing else, and the pawn to c5 hits it at the moment the queen is ready to take back.

The sixth is defence. A knight on g5 and a bishop on c4 are both looking at f7, and one pawn move covers everything.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Answering a hit on the queen with the most active square rather than the safest. Blocking a check with a piece when a pawn does it with tempo. Playing a pawn break before the pieces are placed to take back on it. And meeting an attack on f7 by counting attackers, when the pawn to e6 makes the count irrelevant.}
1. e4 d5 2. exd5 {The capture, which is what nearly every game has. [%weight 9]} ( 2. d3
{Exercise 3 runs through chapter 4's 2.d3 line and one move further. [%weight 3]}
2... dxe4 {Take. [%tags development]} 3. dxe4 {White takes back. [%weight 9]} 3... Qxd1+
{Trade with check. [%tags endgame]} 4. Kxd1 {The king takes back. [%weight 10]} 4... Nc6
{Develop. [%tags development]} 5. Nf3 {Develop. [%weight 8]} 5... Bg4
{The pin. [%tags light-squares]} 6. Be2 {Breaking the pin. [%weight 7]} 6... O-O-O+
{Castle long with check. [%tags king-safety]} 7. Ke1
{The only square: c1 has the bishop and e2 has the other one. Black to play, and there is a knight jump. [%weight 10]}
7... Nb4
{The jump. The knight threatens the fork on c2, which hits the king and the rook on a1, and with the queens off White has no easy way to cover the square. [%plan If White covers c2 with the king or a knight, take on f3 and double the pawns, or bring the knight to d5 and play the ending a move ahead. If White does not, take on c2 and then the rook.] [%line Exercise 3: the knight jump to b4]}
( 7... Bxf3?!
{8.Bxf3 and the pin is gone for nothing, with White's bishop now on the long diagonal.} )
( 7... Nd4?
{8.Nxd4 and the knight is lost: the pin on f3 is only to the bishop, and 8...Bxe2 9.Nxe2 leaves White a piece up.}
) ) ( 2. Nf3
{Exercise 6 is the Tennison Gambit from chapter 5, to the moment two pieces look at f7. [%weight 5]}
2... dxe4 {Take. [%tags development]} 3. Ng5 {The knight eyes f7. [%weight 7]} 3... Nf6
{Defend the pawn. [%tags development]} 4. Bc4
{The bishop joins in. Black to play: two pieces on f7, and one pawn move answers both. [%weight 8]}
4... e6
{The pawn covers f7 and d5 at once. Counting attackers does not matter once the square has a pawn in front of it, and the knight on g5 is now the piece with nothing to do. [%tags king-safety] [%plan Develop the bishop to e7, castle, and let White take the pawn on e4 back with the knight while every Black piece comes out. Chapter 5 has the line.] [%line Exercise 6: f7 defended by a pawn]}
) 2... Qxd5 {The queen takes back. [%tags queen-safety]} 3. Nc3
{The hit on the queen. [%weight 9]} 3... Qd6 {The modern square. [%tags queen-safety]}
4. d4 {The centre. [%weight 8]} 4... Nf6 {Develop. [%tags development]} 5. Nb5
{Exercise 1. The knight hits the queen and eyes c7, where a fork of the king and rook is waiting. Black to play. [%weight 5]}
( 5. Bb5+
{Exercise 2. The check. Black to play, and there is a block that comes with tempo. [%weight 4]}
5... c6
{Block with the pawn that was going to c6 anyway. It hits the bishop, which has to move a second time, and the structure is a move further on for free. [%tags queen-safety]}
( 5... Bd7?!
{It blocks with a piece the structure wanted elsewhere, and 6.Bxd7+ Nbxd7 has traded off the bishop that belongs on f5 or g4.}
) ( 5... Nbd7?! {The pin stays on and the knight is stuck on d7 until it is broken.} )
6. Ba4 {The bishop retreats, keeping the pin along the diagonal for later. [%weight 7]}
6... Bf5
{The bishop comes out before the pawn goes to e6, as always. [%tags light-squares]} (
6... e6?! {Shut in. The bishop comes out first.} ) ( 6... b5!?
{It hits the bishop again, which is fine, and it commits the queenside pawns before the pieces are out.}
) 7. Nf3 {Develop. [%weight 8]} 7... e6
{The pawn goes with the bishop out. [%tags development]} ( 7... Nbd7!?
{Fine. The pawn first frees the bishop on f8.} ) 8. O-O {White castles. [%weight 8]}
8... Be7 {Develop. [%tags development]} ( 8... Nbd7!?
{Fine. The bishop first keeps castling one move away.} ) 9. Re1
{The rook comes to the open file. [%weight 7]} 9... O-O
{Castle. The check cost White a tempo and Black has the full structure. [%tags king-safety] [%plan Knight to d7, queen to c7, and the pawn to c5. The bishop on a4 is looking at nothing and will have to come back to trade.] [%line Exercise 2: the check blocked with tempo]}
( 9... Nbd7!? {Fine. The king first, because nothing is waiting.} ) ) ( 5. Nf3
{Develop. [%weight 8]} 5... c6 {The structure. [%tags queen-safety]} 6. Be2
{Exercise 4 runs through chapter 0's line and one move further. [%weight 5]} ( 6. Bd3
{Exercise 5 runs through chapter 1's 6.Bd3 line and two moves further. [%weight 5]}
6... Bg4 {The pin. [%tags light-squares]} 7. h3 {The question. [%weight 8]} 7... Bh5
{Keep the pin. [%tags light-squares]} 8. O-O {Castle. [%weight 9]} 8... e6
{The pawn goes. [%tags development]} 9. Re1 {The rook. [%weight 7]} 9... Be7
{Develop. [%tags development]} 10. Ne4 {The knight jumps in. [%weight 7]} 10... Nxe4
{Trade it.} 11. Bxe4 {The bishop takes back. [%weight 9]} 11... O-O
{Castle. [%tags king-safety]} 12. c3 {White props up d4. [%weight 7]} 12... Nd7
{The knight comes out to d7, where it covers e5 and c5 and is ready for either break. [%tags development]}
( 12... Qc7!?
{The queen moves before it is asked. The knight first, so that the break has a piece behind it.}
) ( 12... f5?!
{It hits the bishop and weakens e6 for good, and 13.Bd3 leaves Black with a hole and a bishop on h5 that has lost its retreat.}
) 13. Qe2
{The queen comes off the file the pin is on. Black to play, and the bishop on e4 is standing in the middle of the board. [%weight 7]}
13... Nf6
{The knight comes back with a hit on the bishop. It went to d7 to cover the break squares, and now the bishop on e4 has to move so that the knight can have the centre. [%tags development]}
( 13... Qc7!?
{Sound, and the queen has not been asked to move. The knight move gains the tempo first.}
) ( 13... Rad8!?
{Also fine. The knight first, because the bishop on e4 moves with tempo now and for free later.}
) 14. Bc2
{The bishop drops back to the diagonal it wants. Every White piece is placed, and the d-pawn has the c-pawn behind it and nothing else. Black to play: the structure was built for one pawn move. [%weight 7]}
14... c5
{The break. It hits d4 at the moment the queen on d6 is ready to take back on c5, and the pawn on c3 cannot hold d4 on its own. The centre opens for Black's pieces. [%tags c5-break] [%plan If White takes on c5, take back with the queen, which stands in the centre and goes to c7 when the bishop hits it. If White holds d4, put the rooks on d8 and c8 and keep the tension until it breaks in Black's favour.] [%line Exercise 5: the break with the c-pawn]}
( 14... Rad8!?
{Sound, and it lets White play b4 and keep the c-pawn at home. The break was ready and this delays it.}
) ( 14... Qc7!?
{The queen moves before it is asked to. The break was ready and this delays it a move.} )
) 6... Bf5 {Out before e6. [%tags light-squares]} 7. O-O {Castle. [%weight 8]} 7... e6
{The pawn goes. [%tags development]} 8. Bg5 {The pin. [%weight 6]} 8... Be7
{Break it by developing. [%tags development]} 9. Qd2 {Connecting. [%weight 6]} 9... Nbd7
{The last piece. [%tags development]} 10. Rad1 {A rook behind the d-pawn. [%weight 6]}
10... O-O {Castle. The structure is complete. [%tags king-safety]} 11. Rfe1
{The other rook comes to the centre. Every White piece is placed and so is every Black one, and the bishop on g5 is still pinning the knight to the bishop on e7. Black to play: the pin is the last thing to deal with. [%weight 7]}
11... Nd5
{The knight to d5. It steps out of the pin, offers the bishop a trade on e7 that suits Black, and hits the knight on c3. The queen on d6 stops being the piece the bishop points at. [%tags development]}
( 11... c5?!
{The break comes a move early. 12.Nh4 hits the bishop, and after 12...cxd4 13.Nxf5 exf5 14.Qxd4 the queens come off and White's pieces are the freer ones.}
) ( 11... h6!?
{Also fine. It asks the bishop the question a move earlier; the knight move asks it from the centre.}
) ( 11... Qb4!?
{Sound, offering the queen trade and eyeing b2. The knight move keeps the queens on and deals with the pin first.}
) 12. Bxe7 {White trades rather than retreat. [%weight 7]} 12... Qxe7
{Take back with the queen, which leaves the d-file and the bishop's old diagonal in one move. The knight on d5 is the best piece on the board. [%tags queen-safety] [%plan If White takes on d5, take back with the c-pawn and put a rook on c8 against the half-open file. Otherwise the knight stays on d5, the rooks come to the centre, and the game is level.] [%line Exercise 4: the knight to d5]}
( 12... Nxe7!?
{Also fine. The queen recapture takes the queen off the file White's rook is on and keeps the knight on its square.}
) ( 12... Qf4?
{It hopes for the queen trade on d2, but 13.Nxd5 Qxd2 14.Rxd2 cxd5 15.Bxf8 and the bishop has taken a rook.}
) ) 5... Qd8
{Home to d8, which covers c7. The knight on b5 now has nothing to do there and will have to go back, so the tempo is given and taken back at once. [%tags queen-safety] [%plan Play the pawn to a6 to send the knight back, then c6, and develop as usual. The queen comes out again to c7 once the knight has gone.] [%line Exercise 1: the knight on b5]}
( 5... Qd5? {It leaves c7: 6.Nxc7+ Kd8 7.Nxa8 and White has taken a rook.} ) ( 5... Qb6!?
{Sound, and it also covers c7 while hitting b2. It gives White the a-pawn to chase it again, which is why the quieter square is the course's.}
) ( 5... Qe6+?!
{6.Be3 blocks with a developing move and c7 is still hanging, so the queen has to move again.}
) *
